PNT.registerModule({"id": "m03", "number": 3, "title": "GNSS Signals, Errors and Performance", "subtitle": "", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Identify the principal contributors to a GNSS pseudorange error budget.", "Explain why GNSS systems transmit multiple signals and frequencies, at a conceptual level.", "Describe ionospheric and tropospheric propagation effects and how they differ.", "Explain multipath, non-line-of-sight reception and signal obstruction.", "Recognise satellite orbit/clock, receiver and instrumental contributions to measurement error.", "Explain the difference between code pseudorange and carrier-phase measurements.", "Explain Dilution of Precision (DOP) and why geometry amplifies rather than creates measurement error.", "Distinguish GDOP, PDOP, HDOP, VDOP and TDOP at a practical level.", "Connect measurement quality and geometry to accuracy, availability, continuity and integrity.", "Ask meaningful engineering questions about claimed GNSS performance."], "lessons": [{"id": "from-a-gnss-fix-to-a-trustworthy-gnss-fix", "title": "From a GNSS Fix to a Trustworthy GNSS Fix", "html": "<p>Module 2 followed the receiver from satellite signal to a Position, Velocity and Time (PVT) solution. A receiver can acquire satellites, decode navigation data, form pseudoranges and successfully solve for position and clock bias, yet the resulting solution can still contain significant error.</p>\n<p>This module explains why. We will treat every pseudorange as a measurement with an error budget, examine where those errors originate, and then see how satellite geometry controls the way measurement uncertainty becomes position and time uncertainty.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The central question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">If the receiver has four or more satellites and the mathematics produces a solution, what determines how good that solution actually is?</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m03-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Identify the principal contributors to a GNSS pseudorange error budget.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why GNSS systems transmit multiple signals and frequencies, at a conceptual level.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe ionospheric and tropospheric propagation effects and how they differ.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain multipath, non-line-of-sight reception and signal obstruction.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise satellite orbit/clock, receiver and instrumental contributions to measurement error.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the difference between code pseudorange and carrier-phase measurements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain Dilution of Precision (DOP) and why geometry amplifies rather than creates measurement error.</p></li>\n<li><p>Distinguish GDOP, PDOP, HDOP, VDOP and TDOP at a practical level.</p></li>\n<li><p>Connect measurement quality and geometry to accuracy, availability, continuity and integrity.</p></li>\n<li><p>Ask meaningful engineering questions about claimed GNSS performance.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m03-what-we-are-deliberately-not-covering-yet\">What we are deliberately not covering yet</h2>\n<p>Module 4 will cover augmentation and high-accuracy techniques such as differential GNSS, SBAS, RTK and PPP. Modules 6 and 7 address deliberate interference, spoofing and resilient PNT. This module concentrates on nominal GNSS signal and measurement performance, although interference is acknowledged where necessary.</p>"}, {"id": "the-gnss-measurement-error-budget", "title": "The GNSS Measurement Error Budget", "html": "<p>The pseudorange introduced in Module 2 is not simply the true satellite-to-receiver distance. It is a measurement containing the desired geometric range plus several additional effects.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Measured pseudorange = geometric range + clock effects + propagation effects + equipment effects + multipath + noise</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Error / effect</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Where it originates</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why it matters</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Satellite orbit error</td>\n<td>Imperfect knowledge/prediction of satellite position</td>\n<td>If the satellite is not where the receiver believes it is, the geometry used in the solution is wrong.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Satellite clock error</td>\n<td>Difference between satellite clock behaviour and system time after applied corrections</td>\n<td>A timing error maps directly into apparent range error.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Receiver clock bias</td>\n<td>Receiver clock is not perfectly aligned to GNSS system time</td>\n<td>Estimated as part of the PVT solution, but remains central to measurement modelling.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Ionospheric delay</td>\n<td>Charged upper atmosphere</td>\n<td>Changes signal propagation; effect depends strongly on frequency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Tropospheric delay</td>\n<td>Neutral lower atmosphere</td>\n<td>Refraction/delay influenced by atmospheric conditions and elevation angle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Multipath / NLOS</td>\n<td>Reflection or indirect propagation near the receiver</td>\n<td>The measured path can be longer or distorted relative to direct line of sight.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Receiver / instrumental effects</td>\n<td>Antenna, RF chain, electronics and processing</td>\n<td>Hardware delays, thermal noise and implementation quality affect measurements.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The GNSS Measurement Error Budget\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m03-image1.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineering mindset</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A position error is usually the result of both measurement errors and the geometry through which those errors are mapped into the navigation solution. Do not treat 'GPS accuracy' as a single fixed number.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "gnss-signals-and-frequencies", "title": "GNSS Signals and Frequencies", "html": "<p>GNSS constellations transmit structured signals on defined radio-frequency bands. Different constellations use different signal designs and naming conventions, but engineers will commonly encounter terms such as GPS L1, L2 and L5 and Galileo E1, E5 and E6.</p>\n<p>For this course, the important point is not memorising every signal. It is understanding why multiple signals and frequencies are useful.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Different services and ranging codes can be provided on different signals.</p></li>\n<li><p>Measurements on more than one frequency allow frequency-dependent propagation effects, particularly ionospheric delay, to be estimated or substantially mitigated.</p></li>\n<li><p>Modern signals can provide improved tracking, robustness or measurement characteristics for particular applications.</p></li>\n<li><p>Multi-frequency does not mean multi-constellation: a receiver may use several frequencies from one constellation, several constellations on one or more frequencies, or both.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: GNSS Signals and Frequencies\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m03-image2.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Do not confuse these terms</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Multi-frequency describes use of more than one signal frequency. Multi-constellation describes use of more than one GNSS constellation. Both can improve capability, but neither alone guarantees resilience.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "the-ionosphere", "title": "The Ionosphere", "html": "<p>The ionosphere is a region of the upper atmosphere containing electrically charged particles. GNSS radio signals passing through it do not propagate exactly as they would in a vacuum, creating a delay in code measurements.</p>\n<p>A crucial characteristic is that ionospheric delay is dispersive: its magnitude depends on signal frequency. This is why measurements on two or more suitable GNSS frequencies can be combined to estimate or remove much of the first-order ionospheric effect.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why dual-frequency matters</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A receiver observing the same satellite on different frequencies can exploit the fact that the ionosphere affects those frequencies differently. That provides information about the propagation delay that a single-frequency measurement cannot directly observe in the same way.</div></aside>\n<p>Ionospheric conditions vary with factors including time, location, solar activity and the path through the ionosphere. Low-elevation satellite signals travel through a longer effective atmospheric path and can be more affected.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The Ionosphere\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m03-image3.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "the-troposphere", "title": "The Troposphere", "html": "<p>The troposphere and other parts of the neutral atmosphere also refract and delay GNSS signals. Unlike the ionosphere, the tropospheric effect is effectively non-dispersive across the normal GNSS frequency range, so simply comparing two GNSS frequencies does not remove it in the same way.</p>\n<p>Tropospheric delay is modelled using atmospheric behaviour and the satellite elevation angle. Again, low-elevation signals generally experience a longer atmospheric path and can be more difficult to model accurately.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Ionosphere</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Troposphere</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Upper atmosphere with free electrons</td>\n<td>Neutral atmosphere, concentrated nearer the Earth's surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dispersive: effect depends on frequency</td>\n<td>Effect is broadly non-dispersive at GNSS frequencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Multi-frequency combinations can mitigate the first-order effect</td>\n<td>Usually handled through modelling/estimation rather than simple dual-frequency cancellation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Affected by solar/space-weather conditions</td>\n<td>Affected by pressure, temperature, humidity and path/elevation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Useful distinction</p><div class=\"callout-body\">If someone says 'dual frequency removes atmospheric error', challenge the statement. It is particularly powerful against first-order ionospheric delay; tropospheric effects still need to be modelled or estimated.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "multipath-nlos-and-obstruction", "title": "Multipath, NLOS and Obstruction", "html": "<p>GNSS works best when the receiver antenna has clear line of sight to satellites. Real environments contain buildings, terrain, vehicles, vegetation and structures that can block or reflect signals.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m03-multipath\">6.1 Multipath</h2>\n<p>Multipath occurs when the receiver obtains the direct signal together with one or more reflected versions. Those additional paths arrive later and can distort the receiver's estimate of signal timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m03-non-line-of-sight-nlos\">6.2 Non-line-of-sight (NLOS)</h2>\n<p>In a severe case the direct path may be blocked and the receiver may track only a reflected or diffracted signal. The apparent propagation path is then longer than the direct geometric range, potentially biasing the measurement significantly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m03-obstruction-and-masking\">6.3 Obstruction and masking</h2>\n<p>A blocked signal may simply become unusable. This can reduce the number of satellites available and, just as importantly, can remove satellites from useful parts of the sky and worsen geometry.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Multipath, NLOS and Obstruction\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m03-image4.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Accuracy versus availability trade-off</p><div class=\"callout-body\">In difficult environments, a receiver may sometimes retain more measurements by accepting lower-quality or reflected signals. That can improve availability while degrading accuracy. Receiver design therefore involves application-dependent trade-offs.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "receiver-noise-and-equipment-effects", "title": "Receiver Noise and Equipment Effects", "html": "<p>Not every error originates in space or the atmosphere. The antenna, cabling, RF front end, oscillator, correlators and receiver algorithms all influence the quality of the final measurements.</p>\n<p>Thermal noise and finite signal strength introduce measurement uncertainty. Antenna placement and characteristics influence susceptibility to reflections and the signals that are actually received. Hardware and signal-processing chains can also introduce instrumental delays.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">System engineering point</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Two receivers using the same constellation at the same location do not necessarily produce identical performance. Antenna design, receiver architecture, algorithms, installation and the surrounding environment all matter.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "code-measurements-and-carrier-phase", "title": "Code Measurements and Carrier Phase", "html": "<p>Module 2 concentrated on code-derived pseudorange because it is the easiest way to understand basic GNSS positioning. GNSS receivers can also measure the phase of the underlying carrier wave.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Code pseudorange</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Carrier phase</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Directly provides an unambiguous code-based apparent range measurement</td>\n<td>Provides a much more precise phase measurement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Comparatively noisier</td>\n<td>Much lower measurement noise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Straightforward conceptually for standalone positioning</td>\n<td>Contains an unknown integer number of whole carrier wavelengths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Widely used for ordinary navigation solutions</td>\n<td>Fundamental to high-precision techniques such as RTK and PPP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Carrier phase is therefore precise but initially ambiguous. The receiver can measure fractional phase very accurately, but it does not initially know how many complete carrier wavelengths lie between satellite and receiver. Resolving or estimating that ambiguity is central to high-precision GNSS.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Code Measurements and Carrier Phase\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m03-image5.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Remember</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Code: relatively noisy but unambiguous. Carrier phase: highly precise but contains an integer ambiguity. Module 4 will show how high-accuracy techniques exploit carrier measurements.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "satellite-geometry-and-dilution-of-precision", "title": "Satellite Geometry and Dilution of Precision", "html": "<p>Even if every pseudorange had the same measurement uncertainty, the resulting position uncertainty would change as the satellites move across the sky. This is a geometry problem.</p>\n<p>When satellites are well distributed around the receiver, their measurements constrain the solution from different directions. When they are clustered together, the same measurement errors can produce a larger uncertainty in the estimated position.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Satellite Geometry and Dilution of Precision\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m03-image6.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m03-what-dop-actually-means\">9.1 What DOP actually means</h2>\n<p>Dilution of Precision (DOP) is a dimensionless geometry factor. At a simplified level, it describes how the satellite-receiver geometry magnifies measurement uncertainty into uncertainty in a particular part of the navigation solution.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Position uncertainty ≈ measurement uncertainty × geometry factor (DOP)</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Critical distinction</p><div class=\"callout-body\">DOP does not create the underlying ranging error. Poor geometry amplifies the effect of existing measurement uncertainty on the estimated solution. A low DOP cannot rescue badly corrupted measurements, and excellent measurements can still be weakened by poor geometry.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m03-common-dop-terms\">9.2 Common DOP terms</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What it describes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GDOP</td>\n<td>Geometric dilution considering position and time together.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PDOP</td>\n<td>Position dilution in three dimensions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>HDOP</td>\n<td>Horizontal position dilution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>VDOP</td>\n<td>Vertical position dilution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>TDOP</td>\n<td>Time dilution.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Lower DOP generally represents stronger geometry; higher DOP represents weaker geometry. However, a DOP number should not be interpreted as a complete accuracy claim because it says nothing about the actual magnitude or type of the measurement errors being multiplied.</p>"}, {"id": "why-vertical-accuracy-is-often-worse", "title": "Why Vertical Accuracy is Often Worse", "html": "<p>GNSS satellites are observed above the user rather than distributed equally above and below. This creates an inherently less favourable geometry in the vertical dimension than in the horizontal plane for many users.</p>\n<p>As a result, VDOP is often larger than HDOP and vertical position uncertainty is commonly worse than horizontal uncertainty. This is one reason a specification should state whether an accuracy requirement is horizontal, vertical or three-dimensional rather than simply saying 'accurate to X metres'.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Why Vertical Accuracy is Often Worse\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m03-image7.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "measurement-quality-geometry-user-performance", "title": "Measurement Quality + Geometry = User Performance", "html": "<p>We can now connect the pieces. The receiver's user-level performance depends on both the quality of its measurements and the geometry of the satellites contributing to the solution.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Scenario</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Measurement quality</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Geometry</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Likely consequence</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Open sky, well-distributed satellites</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Strong nominal solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Open sky, satellites clustered</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n<td>Measurement errors are more strongly amplified.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Urban canyon</td>\n<td>Mixed / poor</td>\n<td>Often poor</td>\n<td>Multipath/NLOS plus restricted sky view can significantly degrade position.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Few usable satellites</td>\n<td>Variable</td>\n<td>Potentially weak</td>\n<td>Solution may degrade or become unavailable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Multi-frequency open-sky receiver</td>\n<td>Potentially improved atmospheric mitigation</td>\n<td>Depends on sky geometry</td>\n<td>Can reduce important measurement errors, but geometry still matters.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">There is no universal 'GNSS accuracy'</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Performance depends on constellation state, signal set, receiver, antenna, atmosphere, environment, geometry, algorithms and any augmentation used. Always ask under what conditions an accuracy figure was obtained.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "reconnecting-to-pnt-performance", "title": "Reconnecting to PNT Performance", "html": "<p>Module 1 introduced accuracy, integrity, availability and continuity. GNSS signal and measurement behaviour affects all of them.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Performance characteristic</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Connection to this module</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n<td>Measurement biases/noise and DOP affect closeness of the PVT solution to truth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>Obstruction, weak signals or insufficient usable geometry may prevent the required solution from being produced.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Continuity</td>\n<td>Changing visibility, tracking loss or sudden degradation can interrupt a previously available service.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>A system must not only produce a solution; for integrity-critical use it must provide appropriate confidence and timely warning when information should not be trusted.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The distinction between accuracy and integrity remains particularly important. A receiver can normally be accurate but occasionally produce an unacceptable error. Whether that error is detected and flagged quickly enough is an integrity question, not merely an average-accuracy question.</p>"}, {"id": "speaking-like-a-pnt-engineer", "title": "Speaking Like a PNT Engineer", "html": "<p>Suppose a supplier states: 'Our receiver is accurate to one metre.' Useful follow-up questions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Is that horizontal, vertical or 3D accuracy?</p></li>\n<li><p>What percentile or statistical confidence does the figure represent?</p></li>\n<li><p>Under what environment: open sky, suburban, urban canyon, indoors or another operating condition?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which constellations, signals and frequencies were used?</p></li>\n<li><p>Was augmentation used?</p></li>\n<li><p>What satellite geometry/DOP conditions applied?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is multipath and NLOS handled?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens when measurement quality deteriorates?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is integrity or confidence in the solution represented to the consuming system?</p></li>\n<li><p>What test evidence and reference truth were used to establish the figure?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">QA and assurance connection</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A performance number without test conditions, statistical definition, reference truth and acceptance criteria is not yet an assurance claim you can meaningfully verify.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "worked-scenario-moving-from-open-sky-into-an-urban-canyon", "title": "Worked Scenario: Moving from Open Sky into an Urban Canyon", "html": "<p>A vehicle begins in open sky using eight well-distributed satellites. Measurements are relatively clean and geometry is strong. As it enters a city street, buildings mask parts of the sky. Some satellites disappear, some direct signals become reflected signals, and the remaining satellites may be concentrated in the visible strip of sky.</p>\n<p>Three things can therefore happen at once: measurement quality degrades through multipath/NLOS; the number of usable measurements falls; and DOP worsens because the geometry becomes less favourable. The receiver may still output a position, but the existence of that fix does not mean its performance is unchanged.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">What should you now ask?</p><div class=\"callout-body\">How does the receiver identify or down-weight poor measurements? What solution-quality indicators are exposed? At what point is the application warned that required performance is no longer being achieved?</div></aside>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>A GNSS solution contains an error budget; pseudorange is not identical to geometric range.</p></li>\n<li><p>Important contributors include satellite orbit/clock effects, ionosphere, troposphere, multipath, receiver/instrument effects and noise.</p></li>\n<li><p>Multi-frequency measurements are particularly valuable because the ionosphere is dispersive.</p></li>\n<li><p>Tropospheric delay remains and is normally modelled or estimated rather than cancelled through simple dual-frequency comparison.</p></li>\n<li><p>Multipath and NLOS can bias measurements, while obstruction can reduce both satellite count and useful geometry.</p></li>\n<li><p>Code measurements are relatively noisy but unambiguous; carrier phase is much more precise but initially ambiguous.</p></li>\n<li><p>DOP describes geometry and how measurement uncertainty is mapped into the solution; it is not itself a ranging error.</p></li>\n<li><p>PDOP, HDOP, VDOP, TDOP and GDOP describe different aspects of dilution.</p></li>\n<li><p>Vertical performance is commonly weaker than horizontal performance because of satellite geometry.</p></li>\n<li><p>A meaningful GNSS performance claim must state conditions, statistics, configuration and evidence.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Error budget</td>\n<td>Structured account of the principal effects contributing uncertainty or bias to a measurement/solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Ionospheric delay</td>\n<td>Frequency-dependent GNSS propagation effect caused by the ionised upper atmosphere.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Tropospheric delay</td>\n<td>GNSS propagation delay through the neutral atmosphere, generally modelled/estimated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Multipath</td>\n<td>Reception of a signal through more than one propagation path, usually including reflections.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>NLOS</td>\n<td>Non-line-of-sight reception, where the direct satellite-to-antenna path is blocked and an indirect signal may be received.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Carrier phase</td>\n<td>Measurement of GNSS carrier-wave phase; highly precise but containing an integer ambiguity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Integer ambiguity</td>\n<td>Unknown whole number of carrier wavelengths in a carrier-phase range measurement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>DOP</td>\n<td>Dilution of Precision: geometry factor relating measurement precision to solution precision.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PDOP</td>\n<td>Position Dilution of Precision for 3D position.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>HDOP</td>\n<td>Horizontal Dilution of Precision.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>VDOP</td>\n<td>Vertical Dilution of Precision.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>TDOP</td>\n<td>Time Dilution of Precision.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GDOP</td>\n<td>Geometric Dilution of Precision, incorporating position and time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Line of sight</td>\n<td>Direct unobstructed propagation path between satellite and receiver antenna.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p>Recommended open-source resources for learners who want to explore GNSS measurement performance in greater depth:</p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: GNSS Basic Observables</strong><br/>\nTechnical explanation of pseudorange, carrier phase and the principal terms in the GNSS measurement model.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Basic_Observables\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Basic_Observables</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: GNSS Measurements Modelling</strong><br/>\nExplains how clock, atmosphere and other delays are represented and corrected in GNSS measurements.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Measurements_Modelling\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Measurements_Modelling</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: Positioning Error</strong><br/>\nUseful reference for satellite geometry and GDOP, PDOP, HDOP, VDOP and TDOP.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Positioning_Error\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Positioning_Error</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: GNSS Measurement Features and Noise</strong><br/>\nDeeper treatment of code and carrier measurement characteristics, receiver noise and multipath.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Measurement_features_and_noise\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Measurement_features_and_noise</a></p>\n<p><strong>GPS.gov: GPS Accuracy</strong><br/>\nAccessible overview of the environmental, geometry and receiver factors that affect real-world GPS accuracy.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gps.gov/gps-accuracy\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gps.gov/gps-accuracy</a></p>\n<p><strong>GPS.gov: GPS Performance Standards &amp; Specifications</strong><br/>\nOfficial GPS Standard Positioning Service performance documentation and supporting standards.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gps.gov/performance-standards-specifications\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gps.gov/performance-standards-specifications</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA/UN: GNSS Educational Curriculum</strong><br/>\nBroader educational curriculum covering measurements, DOP, error budgets, augmentation and GNSS impact factors.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/bookshelf/books/5.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/bookshelf/books/5.pdf</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 04 — HIGH-ACCURACY &amp; AUGMENTED GNSS</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>Module 4 asks how engineers reduce or compensate for the errors introduced here. It will cover multi-frequency and multi-constellation processing in more depth, Differential GNSS (DGNSS), Satellite Based Augmentation Systems (SBAS), Real-Time Kinematic (RTK), Precise Point Positioning (PPP), reference stations, correction data and the trade-offs between accuracy, convergence, coverage, infrastructure and communications.</p>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "Which can contribute to a GNSS pseudorange measurement error?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Ionospheric delay."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Tropospheric delay."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Satellite clock/orbit effects."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Multipath."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Receiver noise."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "All are recognised contributors to the measurement model/error budget."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Why are multiple GNSS frequencies particularly useful for mitigating ionospheric delay?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The ionosphere affects all frequencies identically."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Ionospheric delay is frequency-dependent, allowing suitable multi-frequency measurements to estimate/cancel much of the first-order effect."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The second frequency doubles satellite altitude."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It removes all atmospheric effects."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "The ionosphere is dispersive, so its effect changes with frequency and can be estimated using suitable multi-frequency combinations."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "Which statement about tropospheric delay is most accurate?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "It is removed completely by comparing two GNSS frequencies."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It is broadly non-dispersive at GNSS frequencies and is generally modelled or estimated."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It only occurs above the ionosphere."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It is caused by receiver clock bias."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Tropospheric effects are broadly non-dispersive at GNSS frequencies and normally require modelling or estimation."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "What is multipath?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Receiving only signals from multiple constellations."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Signal propagation along direct and reflected paths that can distort the measurement."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The use of several routes in a navigation application."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Satellite orbit prediction."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Reflected paths change/distort the apparent signal travel time and therefore the range measurement."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "What does DOP primarily describe?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The amount of ionospheric delay."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Receiver thermal noise."}, {"id": "C", "text": "How satellite geometry maps/amplifies measurement uncertainty into solution uncertainty."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The number of GNSS frequencies."}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "DOP is a geometry factor. It does not describe the underlying measurement error itself."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "Which are common DOP terms?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "PDOP."}, {"id": "B", "text": "HDOP."}, {"id": "C", "text": "VDOP."}, {"id": "D", "text": "TDOP."}, {"id": "E", "text": "GDOP."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "These refer respectively to position, horizontal, vertical, time and combined geometric dilution."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "Why is vertical GNSS performance often worse than horizontal performance?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "GNSS satellites normally surround the user equally above and below."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Satellite geometry generally provides weaker vertical constraint because usable satellites are above the horizon."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Receivers do not calculate altitude."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The speed of light is different vertically."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Satellites are above the user, creating less favourable vertical geometry in many situations."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "Which statement best describes carrier-phase measurement?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "It is less precise than code and has no ambiguity."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It is highly precise but initially contains an unknown integer number of wavelengths."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It cannot be used for positioning."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It is the same as HDOP."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Carrier phase offers much greater precision but includes an integer ambiguity that high-accuracy processing must resolve or estimate."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "A vehicle entering an urban canyon may experience which effects?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Increased obstruction."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Multipath or NLOS reception."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Fewer usable satellites."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Worse satellite geometry."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Guaranteed improvement in accuracy."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Urban structures can simultaneously degrade measurement quality, satellite visibility and geometry."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "A supplier states '1 m GNSS accuracy'. What is the best engineering response?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Accept it because accuracy is a fixed property of GNSS."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Ask for the statistical definition, dimension, operating conditions, signal configuration, augmentation and supporting test evidence."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Ask only which GPS model is used."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Convert it to feet and accept the result."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "An accuracy figure is meaningful only when its definition, conditions, configuration and evidence are understood."}]});
